<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>32: Cauchy-Riemann equations</title>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Write the function <m>f(z) = \frac1z</m> as
  <md>
  f(x+iy) = u(x,y) + iv(x,y).
  </md>
  Then verify that <m>f(z)</m> is differentiable and find its
  derivative <m>f'(z)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>u(x,y) = xy</m>.  Find a possible function <m>v(x,y)</m>
  that makes <m>f(z) = u(x,y) + iv(x,y)</m> differentiable.  Can you write
  your function purely in terms of <m>z</m> (rather than <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>)?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Because the complex derivative <m>f'(z)</m> is defined in the same
  way that 
  <m>f'(x)</m> is defined in Calc I (that is,
  <m>
  f(z) - f(z_0) \approx f'(z_0)(z-z_0)
  </m>)
  all the usual rules that you know from Calc I (power rule,
  product rule, quotient rule, chain rule) still hold.</p>
      <p>Find the following derivatives:</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \frac{d}{dz}\left[\frac{z^2 - 1}{z^2 + 1}\right]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\ds \frac{d}{dz}[e^{2z}(z^3 - 1)]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>If we have a complex function <m>f(z) = u(x,y) + iv(x,y)</m>, we can
  define its associated ``Polya vector field'':
  <md>
  F(x,y) = (u(x,y), -v(x,y)).
  </md>
  Explain why <m>f(z)</m> is differentiable if and only if <m>\nabla\cdot F =
  0</m> and <m>\nabla\times F = 0</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>If <m>f(z) = u(x,y) + iv(x,y)</m> is differentiable, explain why
  <m>\nabla u</m> and <m>\nabla v</m> are orthogonal to one another.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
